layout: layouts/base.vto
title: Quickstart
templateEngine: [vento, md]
---

Ten minutes from an empty directory to a running system: a typed example service, a Fresh app that
consumes it through a derived client, Postgres and a shared cache in containers, and an Aspire
dashboard tracing all of it. Every piece you are about to scaffold is one instance of the model
described in [Core concepts](/concepts/) — this page only runs it.

{{ comp callout { type: "note", title: "Prerequisites" } }}
Install <strong><a href="https://docs.deno.com">Deno 2.x</a></strong> and the
<strong><a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/dotnet/aspire/">.NET Aspire CLI</a></strong>, and have
Docker running before continuing. Aspire orchestrates the local resource graph; Postgres and the
shared cache run in containers it starts for you. The .NET dependency is the orchestrator only —
your application code is Deno throughout. <a href="/quickstart/aspire/">Why Aspire, and how to opt
out</a>.
{{ /comp }}

## 1. Install the CLI

```bash
deno install --global --allow-all --name netscript jsr:@netscript/cli{{ releaseSpecifier }}
```

## 2. Scaffold the workspace

```bash
netscript init my-app --db postgres --service --yes
```

The `--service` flag scaffolds the example oRPC service alongside the workspace. `--yes` accepts
the remaining defaults without prompts, including the `dashboard` app name and shared Redis cache
used below.

What lands on disk is the meta-framework as a directory layout:

- `contracts/` — the typed boundary. One versioned definition of every operation;
  [how contracts work](/explanation/contracts/).
- `services/users/` — implements the contract with handlers that are type-locked to it;
  [Services & SDK](/services-sdk/).
- `apps/dashboard/` — the Fresh app, consuming the same contract through a derived client;
  [the web layer](/web-layer/).
- `aspire/` — the generated AppHost that runs everything above as one observed graph;
  [orchestration & runtime](/orchestration-runtime/).

## 3. Start the resource graph

**Nothing runs until this step.**

```bash
cd my-app/aspire
aspire restore   # first run only
aspire start
```

Open the dashboard URL with the one-time login token that `aspire start` prints. Wait until all
four resources report healthy — the Fresh app, the example service, Postgres, and the shared
cache — then open the Fresh app from its resource link. The page heading should read `my-app`.

You are looking at the whole system: click any resource for its logs, and any request for its
trace. This dashboard is the same surface you will use when something breaks —
[observability](/observability/) is wired in from the first minute, not bolted on later.

{{ comp callout { type: "note", title: "Aspire comes before any database command" } }}
The AppHost must start Postgres before a migration, generation, or seed command can connect to it.
Continue with <a href="/data-persistence/how-to/database-migration/">Database migrations</a> when
you need the database workflow.
{{ /comp }}

## 4. Make your first change

Open `apps/dashboard/routes/(_components)/home-view.tsx`. Find the wrapped introductory sentence
that starts with `A generated NetScript workspace` and replace that sentence with
`This checkout dashboard is ours.` Save the file, then refresh the Fresh app URL from Aspire. The
new sentence appears below the `my-app` heading while every resource stays healthy.

A one-line edit is deliberately unspectacular. The point is what you edited: a server-rendered
Fresh view inside a workspace whose service, database, and cache kept running around it. The
interesting changes — adding an operation to the contract and watching its types reach this page —
are the first thing [the Storefront tutorial](/tutorials/storefront/) makes you do.

When you are finished, stop the AppHost from the `aspire/` directory:

```bash
aspire stop --apphost ./apphost.mts
```

